1
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:10,600
Every 30 seconds of a night's sleep gets a label:
Wake, N1, N2, N3 or REM. Experts score them by hand.

2
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:16,100
Can a computer read sleep from one EEG channel, a single pair of scalp electrodes?

3
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:22,000
One pipeline. Input: a single Fpz-Cz EEG channel, 100 samples a second, all night.

4
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:28,200
Process: cut into 30-second epochs, make a frequency picture of each, then a CNN and a BiGRU.

5
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:32,500
Output: one of five stages for every 30 seconds, a hypnogram.

6
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:40,000
A 2026 Scientific Reports paper claims about 83% accuracy from one channel, chaining four ideas.

7
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:44,800
We set out to reproduce it. Within our time and compute, we could not.

8
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:50,800
The paper splits epochs at random, so one person can be on both sides. We split by person.

9
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:56,500
Why? A new patient is a new person; a random split can reward recognising the person.

10
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:04,400
So we changed the question: does it still work on a person
the model has never seen, and can we explain every step?

11
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:12,000
Scope: not a replication or clinical validation. Twenty people, one public dataset, unseen people only.

12
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:19,300
Hypothesis, formed after the baseline failed: nights differ
in signal strength, so per-night scaling should help.

13
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:27,200
Sleep-EDF, public on PhysioNet: healthy adults recorded
at home on cassette recorders, usually two nights each.

14
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:34,700
Trained technicians scored every 30-second page of each
night by hand, using the 1968 Rechtschaffen and Kales rules.

15
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:40,000
Two experts can disagree, so labels are a trusted reference, not perfect truth.

16
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:46,300
We use 20 people, 39 nights, 42,307 epochs. Trimmed, a night is about 1,085 epochs, or 9 hours.

17
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:54,300
Three variables: the EEG signal, the stage label, and a person
ID that only splits the data and never feeds the model.

18
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:01,900
Limits: healthy adults only, just 20 people, human-scored
labels and one channel. This data cannot speak for patients.

19
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:08,800
STFT: a 2-second window slides in 1-second steps, making a 129 × 29 picture of frequency over time.

20
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Why? Stages are defined by rhythms: slow waves mark N3, spindles N2, alpha waves relaxed wake.

21
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:23,500
The paper uses a synchrosqueezed transform. We chose STFT because
we can draw, compute and check it by hand. We never compared them.

22
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:31,500
CNN: three blocks of 32, 64 and 128 channels squeeze each
picture into 128 numbers. Its limit: it sees one epoch at a time.

23
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:38,400
BiGRU: two layers read 20 neighbouring epochs
forwards and backwards, because sleep is a sequence.

24
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:44,400
Why? Sleep comes in long runs: an epoch is easier to judge with its neighbours than alone.

25
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:49,700
The cost: it uses future epochs, so it works offline only, never in real time.

26
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:56,300
Contrastive learning trains the CNN: similar epochs are pulled together, different ones pushed apart.

27
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,800
It is a second training phase with many settings to tune: more time, more compute.

28
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:09,900
We dropped the contrastive stage: reproducing it failed, and we
wanted a chain we can check step by step. We never tested it.

29
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:17,200
Testing is by person: ten folds, 16 people train, 2 pick
the checkpoint, 2 are tested. Nobody appears twice.

30
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:21,500
Only two test people per fold, so scores swing between folds.

31
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:28,900
Baseline first: macro-F1 56.42%, 95% interval 48.26 to 63.44.
Accuracy 65.00%. Folds swing from 39.95% to 74.39%.

32
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:35,800
Opening single nights, three were right on only 7.22%,
18.60% and 19.01% of epochs. The average hid them.

33
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:42,200
To fix this, R1 changes one step: how each frequency picture is scaled before it reaches the CNN.

34
00:03:42,900 --> 00:03:49,500
STFT hands the CNN one picture per epoch. We first rescale
each frequency row, so every frequency counts evenly.

35
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:56,300
That rescaling is a z-score: subtract the mean, divide
by the spread. Values become standard deviations.

36
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:03,300
The baseline already did this, but with one ruler for all nights: the 16 training people's mean and spread.

37
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,300
R1 gives every night its own ruler: that night's own mean and spread, with no labels used.

38
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:16,900
Same architecture, retrained: macro-F1 74.86%, interval 71.33
to 78.03; accuracy 80.99%. Gain +18.44 (10.07 to 27.55).

39
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:21,200
The three failing nights recover to 82.54%, 88.68% and 84.87%.

40
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:28,500
But R1 reads each test night's unlabeled signal and
was chosen after the baseline failed: offline, post-hoc.

41
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:36,600
Whole-recording statistics give 71.84%: −3.02 versus R1,
interval −7.12 to +1.22. No clear difference, not proof of equality.

42
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:43,200
What we cannot say: that it works on new people, since R1 is offline and was chosen after the fact.

43
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:49,800
Nor that 80.99% beats or matches the paper's 83%, that it is clinically ready, or that N1 is solved.

44
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:54,100
None of the 78 audited participants is certified independent.

45
00:04:54,900 --> 00:05:02,000
Nights differ, and an average can hide the one that fails.
Next: lock the protocol, then test on truly independent data.
